# Hadur in ten slides

What it is, where it ranks, what you will build

Building Hadur: Welcome

---

## Robocode

- A programming game: you write a robot in Java, and robots fight on a 2D battlefield
- The engine runs the battle in ticks. Each tick your robot sees, decides, and acts.
- A robot can move, turn its gun and radar, and fire bullets of power 0.1 to 3
- Free, open source, and a good way to learn real Java

---

## The RoboRumble

- A continuous, worldwide tournament for Robocode robots
- Volunteers' machines run pairings all day and upload the scores
- Every robot ends up with a ranking, built from many pairings
- The 1v1 ladder has **1,216** robots. There is also a MeleeRumble for free-for-alls and a TeamRumble for teams of five.

---

## Meet Hadur

**Hadur** (the Hungarian god of war) is a Robocode robot:

- a 1v1 duelist that remembers each opponent across battles
- a melee brain for free-for-alls, and a team of five Hadurs
- a core that has no idea it is inside Robocode

It was designed, written, tested and tuned by Claude agents, with one human, Leigh Griffin, who set the goals and ran the live entries.

---

## Where it stands

The live record for Hadur 3.9, saved 2026-10-07:

| Rank | APS | Survival | Pairings |
|---|---|---|---|
| **13th of 1,216** | **87.15 ± 0.19** | 95.04% | 1,215 |

- 19th in the MeleeRumble and 12th in the TeamRumble, from the same jar
- The highest-ranked robot flying the Irish flag
- Ranks move every day; `docs/strategy-evolution.md` keeps the history

---

## 64th to 13th

| Release | Date | Live APS | Rank |
|---|---|---|---|
| 3.0 | 2026-09-28 | 81.57 | 64th |
| 3.1 | 2026-09-30 | 81.46 | 16th in its first hour, 65th at the end |
| 3.4 | 2026-10-01 | 85.90 | 20th |
| 3.8 | 2026-10-06 | 84.58 | 29th |
| 3.9 | 2026-10-07 | **87.15** | **13th** |

Every stage was gated on a **bench** of real Robocode battles with 95% intervals, and every behaviour is an **EARS requirement** traced to a test. Ideas that benched badly were dropped.

---

## Not by guessing

- Every stage had a **bench gate**: real Robocode battles, run headless, with 95% intervals
- Every behaviour is an **EARS requirement** traced to the tests that prove it
- Ideas that benched badly were dropped on the evidence
- A learnings ledger records what was found, with its evidence

---

## The shape of the code

```mermaid
graph LR
    E[Robocode engine] --> A[Adapter]
    A --> C[Core: no Robocode inside]
    C --> A
    A --> E
```

The strategy lives in a core of plain Java. A thin adapter talks to the engine. That is why the core can be replayed, fuzzed and benched without an engine.

---

## What this course builds

You build **Hadurling**, a small robot of your own, one lab at a time:

- an ordinary Robocode robot at first
- then a core with no Robocode in it, ArchUnit rules, property tests, a replay test
- EARS requirements with a traceability test
- a guess-factor gun, an energy ledger, a saved opponent profile, a bench report

Each lab ends by opening the real Hadur code for the same idea.

It is also a Java course: it starts from a class and a loop, and ends at the level of Hadur's roughly 25,000 lines.

